    question: 'How much does a wood privacy fence cost in St. George?',
    answer: `As of ${COST_AS_OF}, a wood privacy fence runs about ${wood.range} per linear foot installed, based on national published ranges. The one St. George-specific source, a June 2022 HomeBlue estimate, put 4-foot wood privacy at ${localCheck2022.wood} per foot, so a 6-foot cedar fence with gates should be expected toward the upper half of the range.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does a wood fence last in the St. George climate?',
    answer: `Angi estimates ${lifespan.wood.range} for a wood fence, with the species, the finish schedule, and how the posts were set deciding which end you land on. Dry heat and strong sun push unfinished wood toward the short end; a sealed cedar or redwood fence on well-set posts reaches the long end.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Cedar or pressure-treated pine for a St. George fence?',
    answer: 'Cedar and redwood resist rot and insects naturally and move less as they dry, which matters in low humidity; pressure-treated pine costs less and is the common choice for posts and rails. Many fences here use treated posts with cedar boards, which puts the money where the rot risk is highest, at the ground.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How often does a wood fence need to be stained or sealed?',
    answer: `HomeGuide says wood needs sealing every 1 to 2 years, at about ${fenceStain.perLinearFt} per linear foot or ${fenceStain.typical} for a typical 100 to 150 foot fence. In St. George the sun does more damage than rain, so the south- and west-facing runs usually need attention first.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I need a permit for a wood fence in St. George?',
    answer: 'Only in certain cases. St. George City Code §10-18-5 requires a permit for any fence in a front setback; a side or rear-yard fence within the height limits of §10-18-1 (6 feet 4 inches in most zones) generally does not. HOA approval is a separate step and is often required regardless.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I build a solid wood privacy fence in my front yard?',
    answer: 'Usually not. Under §10-18-1, most St. George zones cap front-yard fences at 4 feet, and within 10 feet behind the sidewalk only fences that are at least 50 percent open are allowed. A wood picket or spaced-board style can meet that rule; a solid privacy panel cannot.',
  },
];

const species = [
  { icon: 'fence', title: 'Western red cedar', text: 'The default privacy-fence board in the West. Natural oils resist rot and insects, it is light and stable as it dries, and it takes stain well. Costs more than pine.' },
  { icon: 'frame', title: 'Redwood', text: 'Similar rot resistance and stability to cedar with a deeper color; supply and price vary more. Usually a boards-and-cap choice, not a post choice.' },
  { icon: 'blocks', title: 'Pressure-treated pine', text: 'The low-cost workhorse and the standard for posts and rails that touch concrete and soil. Boards move more as they dry, so expect some checking and cupping if they go up wet.' },
  { icon: 'ruler', title: 'Douglas fir', text: 'Strong and straight, often used for rails and framing. Less rot-resistant than cedar, so it wants a finish and should stay off the ground.' },
];

const desertSpec = [
  { icon: 'sun', title: 'Kiln-dried or seasoned boards', text: 'Lumber that goes up wet shrinks hard in desert air, opening gaps between boards and pulling nails. Ask whether the boards are kiln-dried or at least seasoned before installation.' },
  { icon: 'wrench', title: 'Screws or ring-shank nails, stainless or coated', text: 'Smooth nails back out as boards shrink and swell. Coated or stainless fasteners also avoid the black streaks that plain steel leaves on cedar and redwood.' },
  { icon: 'blocks', title: 'Posts set in concrete, treated or steel', text: 'Posts fail first, at the ground. Pressure-treated posts rated for ground contact, or galvanized steel posts with wood cladding, set in concrete with the top crowned to shed water.' },
  { icon: 'flat', title: 'A rot board at the bottom', text: 'A sacrificial horizontal board along the ground keeps the pickets out of the soil and sprinkler spray. When it rots, it is replaced alone.' },
  { icon: 'frame', title: 'A cap rail on top', text: 'End grain soaks up water and sun damage fastest. A cap rail covers it on the pickets and gives the fence a finished line.' },
  { icon: 'drop', title: 'Finish within the first season', text: 'Sun grays bare wood in months here. A penetrating stain or sealer applied once the wood has dried, then on the HomeGuide 1-to-2-year cycle, is most of what separates a 15-year fence from a 30-year one.' },
];

const styles = [
  { icon: 'fence', title: 'Dog-ear or flat-top privacy', text: 'Pickets butted edge to edge on two or three rails. The standard 6-foot backyard fence and the lowest-cost privacy style. Gaps open as boards shrink.' },
  { icon: 'frame', title: 'Board-on-board (shadowbox)', text: 'Pickets alternate on both sides of the rails, so the fence stays private after the wood shrinks and looks finished from both sides. More lumber, more cost.' },
  { icon: 'flat', title: 'Horizontal', text: 'Long boards run sideways between closely spaced posts. A contemporary look; needs stable, dry lumber and tighter post spacing to avoid sagging.' },
  { icon: 'scroll', title: 'Picket or spaced board', text: 'Open design that can satisfy the city\'s 50-percent-open rule near the sidewalk, where solid panels are not allowed. No privacy.' },
];

const compareSteps = [
  { icon: 'ruler', title: 'Same species, height, style, and footage on every quote', text: 'A cedar board-on-board price and a treated-pine dog-ear price are not comparable. Get the linear footage, height, and gate count in writing.' },
  { icon: 'blocks', title: 'Post material, depth, spacing, and concrete', text: 'Ask what the posts are, how deep they are set, how far apart, and whether every post gets concrete. This is where a low bid saves money and where the fence fails first.' },
  { icon: 'doc', title: 'Fasteners and finish named', text: 'A quote that says "wood fence" without naming the fastener type or whether a first coat of stain is included leaves the two biggest longevity items undecided.' },
  { icon: 'house', title: 'HOA and permit handling', text: 'Confirm who submits the HOA application and, if the fence touches a front setback, who pulls the city permit.' },
