---

<GuideLayout
  title="Wood Privacy Fence, St. George UT"
  description="Wood privacy fence in St. George, UT: cost per foot, cedar vs. treated pine, what desert sun and dry air do to wood, styles, city height and permit rules."
  path={path}
  eyebrow="Material guide · Fences · St. George & Washington County"
  h1="Wood Privacy Fence in St. George: Cost, Species, and Desert Upkeep"
  answer={`As of ${COST_AS_OF}, a wood privacy fence in St. George, UT costs about ${wood.range} per linear foot installed and is estimated to last ${lifespan.wood.range} (Angi), with sealing every 1 to 2 years at roughly ${fenceStain.perLinearFt} per foot. In a city that has hit 117 degrees, the species, the posts, and the first coat of finish decide which end of that life you get.`}
  updated={UPDATED}
  updatedLabel={UPDATED_LABEL}
  article={{ headline: 'Wood Privacy Fence in St. George: Cost, Species, and Desert Upkeep', datePublished: UPDATED }}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { href: '/fence-installation-st-george-ut/', label: 'Fence installation hub', blurb: 'All materials, heights, and permit rules.' },
    { href: '/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/', label: 'Full cost guide', blurb: 'Every range and source.' },
    { href: '/vinyl-fence-installation-st-george-ut/', label: 'Vinyl fence guide', blurb: 'The no-maintenance alternative.' },
    { href: '/cmu-block-wall-vs-wood-fence-st-george-ut/', label: 'Block wall vs. wood fence', blurb: 'If you are weighing a wall instead.' },
    { href: '/fence-repair-st-george-ut/', label: 'Fence repair', blurb: 'Leaning posts, rotted boards, wind damage.' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Wood is still the lowest-cost way to put a solid privacy fence around a St. George backyard, and a well-built cedar fence
    on good posts looks better every year as it weathers. The desert changes the math, though. Low humidity shrinks boards,
    the sun grays and checks bare wood within a season, and wind loads a solid 6-foot panel like a sail. A wood fence here is
    not a worse choice than elsewhere; it is a choice that depends more on how it is built and finished. This guide covers what
    a wood privacy fence costs in St. George, which species hold up, what to specify for heat and dry air, the styles the city
    allows where, the upkeep budget, and how to compare quotes from independent fence companies.
  </p>

  <h2>What a wood privacy fence costs in St. George</h2>
  <CostTable
    caption={`Wood privacy fence cost, as of ${COST_AS_OF}`}
    unit="per linear foot, installed"
    rows={[
      { label: 'Wood privacy, national range', range: wood.range, note: wood.note },
      { label: 'Wood privacy, 4-foot, St. George (HomeBlue, June 2022)', range: localCheck2022.wood, note: 'Older local estimate; cross-check only. Expect 6-foot privacy to run higher.' },
      { label: 'Vinyl (PVC) privacy, for comparison', range: vinyl.range, note: 'Higher up front; no sealing or staining.' },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    Four things move the price inside that range: species (cedar and redwood cost more than pressure-treated pine), height
    (6-foot pickets use half again the lumber of a 4-foot fence and need heavier posts), style (board-on-board uses nearly
    twice the pickets of a dog-ear fence), and gates, which are priced separately and carry the reinforced posts and
    hardware. No St. George fence company publishes prices, so these are dated published ranges, not local quotes; the
    <a href="/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/">cost guide</a> lists every source and date. The price that is easy to
    forget is the finish: HomeGuide puts staining or sealing at {fenceStain.perLinearFt} per linear foot, or
    {fenceStain.typical} for a typical 100 to 150 foot fence, on a 1-to-2-year cycle.
  </p>

  <h2>Which wood holds up in the desert</h2>
  <IconList items={species} cols={2} />
  <p>
    The common St. George combination is pressure-treated posts and rails with cedar pickets and a cedar cap. That puts the
    rot-resistant, ground-rated material where water and soil are, and the stable, better-looking board where you see it.
    An all-cedar fence costs more and is not necessarily longer-lived if the posts are the first thing to fail. An all-treated
    fence is the budget route; it will serve, but expect more movement in the boards and plan the finish from day one.
  </p>

  <h2>Heat, sun, and dry air: what to specify</h2>
  <p>
    St. George tied Utah's all-time high of 117 degrees on July 10, 2021, a reading NOAA's State Climate Extremes Committee
    formally accepted, and ordinary summers run above 100 for weeks with very little humidity. Wood responds to that in three
    ways. It dries and shrinks, which opens gaps between pickets, loosens smooth nails, and cups boards that went up wet.
    Ultraviolet light breaks down the surface of bare wood, turning it gray and raising the grain within a season. And the
    one wet spot on a desert lot, the sprinkler line along the fence, keeps the bottom of the boards damp while the top bakes.
    None of this rules wood out. It makes the build specification the whole decision.
  </p>
  <IconList items={desertSpec} cols={2} />
  <p>
    Wind is the other design load. Solid privacy panels catch it, and the <a href="/fence-repair-st-george-ut/">repair
    guide</a> describes what wind damage looks like after the fact: panels blown out of their rails and posts snapped at the
    ground. Post depth, spacing, and concrete matter more than the picket style, so ask how the company sets posts before you
    compare picket prices.
  </p>

  <h2>Wood fence styles and where the city allows them</h2>
  <IconList items={styles} />
  <p>
    Height and openness are governed by St. George City Code
    <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-1" rel="noopener">§10-18-1</a>. In most zones, front-yard fences are
    capped at 4 feet and side and rear-yard fences at 6 feet 4 inches, and within 10 feet behind the sidewalk only decorative
    fences that are at least 50 percent open are permitted. That rule is why a wood picket works along the street while a
    solid privacy panel does not. Under
    <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-5" rel="noopener">§10-18-5</a>, any fence in a front setback needs
    a permit; the <a href="/fence-permit-st-george-ut/">permit guide</a> covers the application. Washington, Hurricane, Ivins,
    and Santa Clara each have their own code; see the <a href="/washington-county-fence-service-areas/">service area guide</a>.
    Before any post holes are dug, the company should call Blue Stakes of Utah to locate buried utilities.
  </p>

  <h2>HOA rules for wood fences</h2>
  <p>
    Most newer St. George subdivisions are HOA-governed, and wood is where some HOAs draw a hard line: some restrict
    unfinished wood, require a stain color from an approved palette, or allow wood only on interior lot lines and not where a
    fence faces a street, golf course, or open space. It varies by HOA, so always confirm your own CC&amp;Rs. Get the HOA's written
    fence standard before you get quotes. The <a href="/hoa-fence-requirements-st-george-ut/">HOA fence guide</a> explains
    how HOA rules sit on top of city code and what the approval process usually involves.
  </p>

  <h2>The upkeep budget: wood vs. vinyl over the life of the fence</h2>
  <p>
    Wood costs less up front at {wood.range} per foot against vinyl at {vinyl.range}, and that gap is real money on a long
    run. The ongoing cost is the finish. HomeGuide says wood needs sealing every 1 to 2 years at roughly
    {fenceStain.perLinearFt} per linear foot, and Angi puts a wood fence's life at {lifespan.wood.range} against
    {lifespan.vinyl.range} for vinyl. In the desert, skipping the finish is what moves a wood fence to the short end of that
    range, because dry, gray, checked boards split and shed pickets sooner. If you will keep up the stain cycle, wood is a
    sound long-term choice here; if you will not, the <a href="/vinyl-fence-installation-st-george-ut/">vinyl fence guide</a>
    covers the alternative, and the <a href="/cmu-block-wall-vs-wood-fence-st-george-ut/">block wall vs. wood comparison</a>
    adds the masonry option for lots that need a wall anyway.
  </p>

  <h2>Repair or replace an older wood fence?</h2>
  <p>
    Wood fences fail from the ground up. Individual pickets and a rot board are cheap to replace, and HomeGuide's published
    repair ranges start at {repair.minor} for minor work, with a single post replacement around {repair.post}. When several
    posts are rotted at the base or the rails have gone soft, a repair becomes a rebuild in pieces, and a new fence on new
    posts is usually the better spend. The <a href="/fence-repair-st-george-ut/">fence repair guide</a> walks through the
    repair-versus-replace decision and what each type of damage looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>How to compare wood fence quotes</h2>
  <IconList items={compareSteps} />
  <p>
    Confirm the company's Utah license status with the
    <a href="https://secure.utah.gov/llv/search/index.html" rel="noopener">DOPL license lookup</a> and ask for proof of
    insurance; the <a href="/licensed-fence-contractor-compare-st-george-ut/">license guide</a> explains what the record
    shows and the small-project exemption. If you want help finding independent local companies to quote, the
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> describes how matching works.
  </p>

  <p class="sgfw-note">
    Sources: NerdWallet, <a href="https://www.nerdwallet.com/home-ownership/home-improvement/learn/cost-to-install-a-fence" rel="noopener">"Cost to Install a Fence"</a> (updated July 10, 2025);
    HomeBlue, <a href="https://www.homeblue.com/fence-installation/saint-george-ut-fence-cost.htm" rel="noopener">"Fence Cost in Saint George, Utah"</a> (June 28, 2022; cross-check only);
    Angi, <a href={lifespan.wood.url} rel="noopener">"How Long Does a Wood Fence Last?"</a> ({lifespan.wood.date}) and
    <a href={lifespan.vinyl.url} rel="noopener">"How Long Does a Vinyl Fence Last?"</a> ({lifespan.vinyl.date});
    HomeGuide, <a href="https://homeguide.com/costs/cost-to-stain-paint-fence" rel="noopener">"Cost to Stain or Paint a Fence"</a> (updated February 4, 2026) and
    <a href="https://homeguide.com/costs/fence-repair-cost" rel="noopener">"Fence Repair Cost"</a> (updated February 20, 2026);
    NOAA NCEI, <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/monitoring-content/extremes/scec/reports/20220708-Utah-Maximum-Temperature.pdf" rel="noopener">State Climate Extremes Committee report, Utah maximum temperature</a> (July 2022);
    Blue Stakes of Utah, <a href="https://www.bluestakes.org/" rel="noopener">bluestakes.org</a>;
    St. George City Code <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-1" rel="noopener">§10-18-1</a> and
    <a href="https://stgeorge.municipal.codes/Code/10-18-5" rel="noopener">§10-18-5</a> (checked September 26, 2026).
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
